ate v. Doe – 2-Hour Hearing Requested). Ex Parte Communications: Please note that the Code of Judicial Conduct governing behavior by judges prohibits the judges of the Eighth Judicial Circuit from discussing pending cases with the public and prohibits judges from talking to any party, without all other parties present. Please do not call or email the Court expecting to speak with a Judge about any case. If you have a comment or request to make, please file it in writing with the Clerk’s Office and serve a copy on all other parties. Please keep in mind that the Court is only allowed to consider arguments made in court and documents filed by actual parties to the case. Judges cannot ethically read or consider any other opinions or arguments about the case, and they cannot give legal advice. Unsolicited Communications: Unsolicited communications from non-parties will not be considered by the Court. Parties may only contact the judicial office in accordance with these practices and procedures. E-Filing Portal Contact Information: All attorneys and self-represented litigants must provide an e-mail address to receive signed orders electronically, unless excused. Fla. R. Gen. Prac. & Jud. Admin. 2.516. It is the responsibility of attorneys and self-represented litigants to update their contact information using Form 2.603 any time there is a change in the e-mail account registered for electronic service. Scheduling Procedures Court Schedule: Master Calendar for Alachua Circuit Civil & Family Master Calendar for Alachua Criminal – for Veterans Treatment Court Courtroom: All hearings are held in Courtroom 3B or via Zoom. ID Requirement: If a party or witness will provide testimony at the scheduled hearing, the Court must receive by email to wablekc@circuit8.org , a photograph copy of their driver’s license or photo ID no later than three (3) business days prior to the hearing. Filing a Motion of Scheduling a Hearing: All requests for hearing time must be in the form of a motion and properly filed with the clerk or they will not be accepted by the Court. No hearings will be set until the motion is filed and viewable on the clerk’s website. To obtain a hearing time, contact the judicial assistant by email at wablekc@circuit8.org . Hearings will not be set over the phone. Pro se litigants and counsel of record must be included in emails to the judicial assistant. The email request must include a copy of the motion to be heard and the following information: (1) case number; (2) petitioner’s name and attorney (if represented); (3) respondent’s name and attorney (if represented); (4) length of hearing time requested; (5) title of the motion to be heard; (6) whether the hearing is evidentiary; (7) date mediation was conducted. Providing that all parties agree, non-evidentiary hearings of 30 minutes or less may be conducted by Zoom without prior permission from the Court. Evidentiary hearings, hearings longer than 30 minutes, and hearings in which the parties do not agree to Zoom will be held in person unless the Court grants prior permission for the hearing to be conducted by Zoom. If a party requests that an individual, attorney, or witness be permitted to appear remotely at an in-person hearing, a motion for the individual to appear remotely must be submitted at least seven (7) business days prior to the hearing. The motion must state whether the opposing party agrees or objects to the requested remote appearance. Once a motion or petition has been scheduled, other motions or matters shall NOT be noticed at the time reserved unless prior approval has been obtained from the judicial assistant. Notice of Hearings: Once confirmation of the hearing date has been received by email from the judicial assistant, a notice of hearing shall be prepared by the moving party. The notice of hearing shall include the full title of the motion and filing date, the length of the hearing, and shall advise if the hearing will be conducted in person or via Zoom. Hearing Time: The moving party is responsible for requesting the appropriate amount of hearing time. Parties should presume that each side will be given an equal amount of time. If motions from multiple parties are set during the same hearing time, each moving party is responsible for requesting the appropriate amount of time. If there is a concern that a hearing may exceed the allotted time, either party may request for the Court to allot an equal amount of time to each party at the beginning of the hearing, or the Court may distribute time sua sponte. Parties will be responsible for presenting the entirety of their argument and presentation of evidence within their allotted time. Cancelling Hearings: Please notify the Court at least 24 hours in advance of any cancellations, and a notice of cancellation should be emailed to the judicial assistant at the time the notice of cancellation is filed with the Clerk. If the notice of cancellation is not received at least 24 hours prior to the scheduled hearing, that hearing will remain on the docket and parties are expected to appear. Motions for Re-hearing/Reconsideration: Upon filing said motion, email a copy Court for review. The Court will either rule without a hearing or will advise the moving attorney to schedule a hearing. While an email may suffice in the correction of scrivener’s errors, please remember that emails pointing out errors do not toll the time for rehearing or reconsideration under the Rules. Motions to Withdraw and Substitution of Counsel Pursuant to Florida Rules Procedure, all motions to withdraw or for substitution of counsel must contain the client’s signature/consent and specific reason(s) for the withdraw/substitution. Additionally, all proposed orders granting withdrawal shall be accompanied by the filing of an email designation form for the client or notice to the Court that an email designation has been previously filed. If an attorney is unable to obtain client consent or an email designation filled out by the client, then the motion to withdraw or to substitute counsel may be set for hearing. Motions to withdraw filed after the case has been set for trial may not be granted, and all such orders shall include notice to the now pro se litigant of the date and time of the previously scheduled hearing or trial. Motions to withdraw, even with consent, may require a hearing if filed prior to an already scheduled hearing or trial Remote Appearance Zoom Videoconference: For all virtual hearings by Zoom, parties must use both audio and video. If the parties do not have video capability, the matter must be set for an in-person hearing. Proper courtroom decorum should be maintained in virtual hearings. Participants should be in a quiet setting and minimize any external distractions and dress as if they were coming to the courthouse. Preparation and communication are keys to successful remote hearing. Counsel and/or pro se litigants should communicate with each other prior to the hearing and attempt to resolve as many issues as possible outside of court. Prior to the hearing date, it is important to confirm that Zoom technology is operable and that the connection allows for proper performance. Submission of Orders and Judgments Orders: All proposed orders should be sent in Microsoft Word format (unless it is an agreed final judgment with attachments). If the Court requests counsel to prepare an order at a hearing, counsel will prepare and submit the order to the Court via email within seven (7) business days unless a different deadline is set by the Court at the hearing. Counsel shall provide the proposed order to opposing counsel/pro se litigant for approval before submitting it to the Court. Once it has been agreed upon, the order shall be emailed to the Court and advising that the order has been agreed upon. If the parties cannot agree on the form of the order and both parties are represented, the lawyers shall email one proposed order with their difference redlined to the judicial assistant. If the differences in the proposed Orders are so significant that a redline is not practical, the lawyers may submit two separate proposed orders. If one or both parties are unrepresented, then each side shall email their proposed order to the judicial assistant for the Court to review. The Court will render one Order after review. If the other side does not respond timely to a request for approval of a proposed order, the proposed order shall be emailed to the judicial assistant with the explanation that the time frame has lapsed and opposing counsel was unresponsive. All orders should be titled with the name of the motion, the date the hearing was held, and include a complete certificate of service. How to Redline: See Jack Lloyd, How to Redline a Document in Microsoft Word (last updated June 4, 2020) available at How to Redline a Document in Microsoft Word: 2 Easy Ways Proposed Orders: Proposed Orders shall be emailed to wablekc@circuit8.org in Word format .  Please do not send Proposed Orders, Motions, or supporting documentation by United States Mail. All orders shall include a certificate of service at the bottom of the order.  Please indicate the name of the party who is to receive the order, as well as that party’s e-mail/e-service address OR mailing address, if a hard copy is required. Proposed Orders are not accepted via e-filing. Motions should continue to be e-filed with the Clerk’s Office. Support or Income Withholding Orders: When submitting an order or final judgment directing a party to make payments to the State Disbursement Unit, please submit an income deduction order (with attached income withholding order if there is child support) to be entered simultaneously by the Court. It is the responsibility of the receiving party to ensure the obligor’s employer receives a copy of the withholding order pursuant to statute. Case Law Cases being relied upon by the parties at a hearing or trial should be provided to opposing counsel and the Court no later than three (3) business days prior to the hearing. However, the Court will always consider any law applicable to the matters being litigated. Litigants should highlight the parts of a law or case that they believe are pertinent to the issues at hand. All other non-exhibit documents that a party wishes the Court to review must be provided to the Court and the opposing party by email to wablekc@circuit8.org at least three (3) business days prior to the hearing or trial. Each case or document should be provided as a separate file. Please confer with all parties prior to the hearing to determine whether there are any stipulations as to the admissibility of evidence. Any pre-marked evidence which is stipulated should be so noted. All other pre-marked evidence will be considered to be opposed. Emergency and Other Urgent Matters A party requesting emergency action must include the words “Emergency Motion” in the title of the motion and must set forth in detail the nature of the emergency, the date by which a ruling is necessary, and the reason the ruling is needed by the stated date. The emergency motion process should only be used in situations where a child is threatened with harm or where a party plans to improperly remove a child from the state. See Loudermilk v. Loudermilk , 693 So. 2d 666 (Fla. 2d DCA 1997). If one of these circumstances exists or another that rises to the level that would require immediate action to prevent harm, a party may file an emergency motion. The unwarranted designation of a motion as an emergency motion may result in sanctions. All emergency motions must be verified. Emergency motions must be e-mailed to the judicial assistant for the Court’s review. If the motion fails to raise an actual emergency, it will be denied to the extent that it seeks emergency relief (but not as to the merits) and may be scheduled to be heard in the ordinary course. A party whose time-sensitive motion does not qualify as an emergency motion but who nonetheless requires an expedited ruling by a date certain may file an expedited motion. An expedited motion must include the words “Expedited Motion” in the title and must set forth in detail the date by which an expedited ruling is needed and the reason the ruling is needed by the stated date. As a general matter, an expedited motion is one that will become moot if not ruled upon by a date certain. Parties are reminded that difficulties with timesharing or other problems that are not objectively extremely serious and imminent are NOT emergencies. Exhibits for Evidentiary Proceedings If you intend to present any evidence or documents to the court at your hearing, you must pre-mark all items for identification (ex. “Petitioner’s Exhibit A, B, C..”) and serve a copy via Certified US Mail to all parties no less than ten (10) business days prior to the hearing. Digital Exhibits that cannot be sent via US Mail (i.e., videos, flash drives, etc.) must be included on the exhibit list (and properly marked) and hand delivered directly to the opposing counsel or party no later than ten (10) business days prior to the hearing. In addition, a paper copy of all pre-marked exhibits, along with an Index of Exhibits from each side, shall be received by the Court via Certified US Mail no later than the deadline contained in your specific order, or five (5) business days prior to the hearing date. This packet will be used by the Court to stamp the official evidence as it is entered at the hearing/trial. The Court will not accept electronic devices (i.e., videos, flash drives, etc.) sent via US Mail; you may submit a physical copy at hearing/trial. Please confer with all parties prior to the hearing to determine whether there are any stipulations as to the admissibility of evidence. Any pre-marked evidence which is stipulated, should be so noted. All other pre-marked evidence will be considered opposed. Evidence should not be filed with the Clerk. Forms Forms can b